Run threads on parked workers

Each spawn starts a Web Worker, which loads the module's shim and
instantiates the module before the thread runs, and a spawn from a worker
waits on the main thread to start it, behind whatever that thread is busy
with. Here a worker whose thread has finished parks, and the next spawn
with the same worker script and stack size, from any thread, hands its
thread to the worker parked last. A worker parks before its thread's
joiner is woken, so a spawn right after a join finds it. A scoped thread
is counted before it is handed over, since a parked worker can finish it
before the spawn returns. A reused worker keeps the name and thread-locals
of the thread it first ran.

A parked worker is kept for the rest of the page's life, since a worker's
exit can stall the page's main thread, as it does in Firefox under its
profiler, and a thread spawned later would start cold. Each costs its
stack and a few megabytes, so no more workers park than the browser
reports logical processors, enough for the threads of two of bviz's
tasks, which each fan out to half of them; a worker whose thread finishes
beyond that ends.

A thread must leave nothing queued on its worker's event loop, such as a
promise, a timer or a message handler: a parked worker does not return to
the loop, so that work would never run.

A thread handed to a parked worker that does not take it up within a grace
period, as when the browser has ended the worker, for example for running
out of memory, is taken back and started on a new worker, which has no
name; the parked worker ends if it wakes.

